hadiths[4]{hadithnumber,text}:
"16949","Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, Hariz narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Sulayh, from Dhu Mikhmar, and he was a man from Abyssinia who served the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: We were with him on a journey, and he hastened the march when he departed, and he used to do that due to lack of provisions. Then one said to him: O Messenger of Allah, the people have been cut off behind you. So he halted, and the people halted with him until they all caught up to him. Then he said to them: Would you like to rest a nap? Or one said to him. So he alighted and they alighted. Then he said: Who will guard us tonight? So I said: I, may Allah make me your ransom. Then he gave me the halter of his she-camel. He said: Here, do not be a lukaa. He said: So I took the halter of the she-camel of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and the halter of my she-camel, and I withdrew not far, and I let them graze. While I was thus looking at them, sleep overcame me, and I felt nothing until I found the heat of the sun upon my face. So I awoke and looked right and left, and behold the two mounts not far from me. So I took the halter of the she-camel of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and the halter of my she-camel, and I came to the nearest of the people and woke him. So I said to him: Have you prayed? He said: No. Then the people woke one another until the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, awoke. Then he said: O Bilal, is there water for ablution with you, meaning the water-skin? He said: Yes, may Allah make me your ransom. Then he brought him ablution water, so he performed ablution, not rubbing dust from it. Then he ordered Bilal, so he called the adhan. Then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stood and prayed the two rakas before dawn, unhurried. Then he ordered him, so he established the prayer, and he prayed unhurried. Then one said to him: O Prophet of Allah, we were negligent. He said: No, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, took our souls, and He has returned them to us, and we have prayed."
"16950","Rawh narrated to us, al-Awzai narrated to us, from Hassan ibn Atiya, from Khalid ibn Madan, from Dhu Mikhmar, a man among the companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: The Byzantines will make a secure truce with you, then you and they will fight an enemy beyond them, and you will be aided and safe and take spoils. Then you will aid the Byzantines until you alight at a meadow with hills. Then a man from Christendom will raise a cross and say: The cross has prevailed. Then a man from the Muslims will become angry, stand to him, and break it. At that, the Byzantines will betray and gather for the battle."
"16951","Muhammad ibn Musab, he is al-Qirqisai, narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, from Hassan ibn Atiya, from Khalid ibn Madan, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Dhu Mikhmar, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: You will make a secure truce with the Byzantines, and you and they will fight an enemy beyond them, and you will be safe and take spoils. Then you will alight at a meadow with hills. Then a man from the Byzantines will stand to it and raise the cross and say: Verily the cross has prevailed. Then a man from the Muslims will stand to him and kill him. At that, the Byzantines will betray, and the battles will be. Then they will gather against you and come to you in eighty banners, with every banner ten thousand."
"16952","Abd al-Quddus Abu al-Mughira narrated to us, he said: Hariz, meaning Ibn Uthman al-Rahabi, narrated to us, he said: Rashid ibn Sad al-Maqrai narrated to us, from Abu Hayy, from Dhu Mikhmar, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: This rule was in Himyar, then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, removed it from them and placed it in Quraysh, and it will return to them. And thus it was in the book of my father, spaced out, and where he narrated it to us he spoke upon equality."
